23002</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1093/bioinformatics/btp352</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sievers, F. &amp; Higgins, D. G. Clustal Omega for making accurate alignments of many protein sequences. Protein Sci. 27, 135&#x2013;145 (2018).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28884485</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1002/pro.3290</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Capella-Guti&#xe9;rrez, S., Silla-Mart&#xed;nez, J. M. &amp; Gabald&#xf3;n, T. trimAl: a tool for automated alignment trimming in large-scale phylogenetic analyses. Bioinformatics 25, 1972&#x2013;1973 (2009).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19505945</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">2712344</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1093/bioinformatics/btp348</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Nguyen, L.-T., Schmidt, H. A., von Haeseler, A. &amp; Minh, B. Q. IQ-TREE: a fast and effective stochastic algorithm for estimating maximum-likelihood phylogenies. Mol. Biol. Evol. 32, 268&#x2013;274 (2015).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">25371430</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1093/molbev/msu300</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kalyaanamoorthy, S., Minh, B. Q., Wong, T. K., von Haeseler, A. &amp; Jermiin, L. S. ModelFinder: fast model selection for accurate phylogenetic estimates. Nat. Methods 14, 587&#x2013;589 (2017).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/nmeth.4285</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28481363</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">5453245</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Xie, J. et al. Tree Visualization By One Table (tvBOT): a web application for visualizing, modifying and annotating phylogenetic trees. Nucleic Acids Res. 51, W587&#x2013;W592 (2023).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">37144476</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">10320113</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1093/nar/gkad359</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Stolzer, M. et al. Inferring duplications, losses, transfers and incomplete lineage sorting with nonbinary species trees. Bioinformatics 28, I409&#x2013;I415 (2012).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">22962460</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">3436813</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1093/bioinformatics/bts386</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42866584</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>13</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>13</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2730-6151</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>6</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Jan</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>ISME communications</Title><ISOAbbreviation>ISME Commun</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>A novel <i>Thermodesulfovibrionia</i> lineage largely supports sulfate reduction in sandy sediments of the southern Baltic Sea.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>ycag255</StartPage><MedlinePgn>ycag255</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">ycag255</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/ismeco/ycag255</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Sandy sediments of the coastal ocean are generally not considered a habitat for highly active sulfate-reducing microorganisms (SRM) due to fluctuations in oxygen concentrations and carbon availability. This study investigated the taxonomic composition and gene expression patterns of sulfate-reducing communities in both sandy, organic-poor sediments and muddy, organic-rich sediments in the mesohaline southern Baltic Sea. Our results revealed that a novel order-level lineage within the class <i>Thermodesulfovibrionia</i> (phylum <i>Nitrospirota</i>), named <i>Arenidesulfomonadales</i>, contributed significantly to sulfate reduction in the sandy sediments and supported sulfate reduction rates similar to those in the organic-rich, muddy sediments. In the sands, <i>Thermodesulfovibrionia</i> effectively competed with <i>Desulfobacterales</i>, which strongly dominated the sulfate-reducing communities in the organic-rich sediments. Metatranscriptomic data revealed that <i>Desulfobacterales</i> functioned as typical secondary fermenters in both sediment types and expressed nearly complete sets of genes for the catabolism of fermentation products such as lactate, propionate, and butyrate. In contrast, the gene expression profile of <i>Thermodesulfovibrionia</i> suggests that hydrogenotrophic sulfate reduction, coupled with carbon fixation via the Wood-Ljungdahl pathway and a reduced capacity to oxidize organic compounds, was a competitive metabolic strategy enabling sulfate reduction to proceed independently of fermentation-derived carbon. Overall, our results suggest that overlooked lineages of SRM may contribute to higher levels of sulfate reduction than previously assumed in the extensive areas of permeable sediments of coastal and shelf seas.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; The Author(s) 2026. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the International Society for Microbial Ecology.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Piontek</LastName><ForeName>Judith</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-3857-8206</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Biological Oceanography, Leibniz Institute for Baltic Sea Research Warnem&#xfc;nde, Seestr. 15, 18119 Rostock, Germany.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Schott</LastName><ForeName>Thomas</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0007-3989-926X</Identifier>