ane in Mars&#x2019; atmosphere show strong seasonal variations. Science. 2018;360:1093&#x2013;6. doi: 10.1126/science.aaq0131.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1126/science.aaq0131</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">29880682</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Oehler DZ, Etiope G. Methane seepage on Mars: where to look and why. Astrobiology. 2017;17:1233&#x2013;64. doi: 10.1089/ast.2017.1657.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1089/ast.2017.1657</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC5730060</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28771029</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Marlow JJ, Larowe DE, Ehlmann BL, Amend JP, Orphan VJ. The potential for biologically catalyzed anaerobic methane oxidation on ancient Mars. Astrobiology. 2014;14:292&#x2013;307. doi: 10.1089/ast.2013.1078.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1089/ast.2013.1078</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">24684241</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ji M, Greening C, Vanwonterghem I, Carere CR, Bay SK, Steen JA, et al. Atmospheric trace gases support primary production in Antarctic desert surface soil. Nature. 2017;552:400&#x2013;3. doi: 10.1038/nature25014.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/nature25014</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">29211716</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Berg JS, Ahmerkamp S, Pjevac P, Hausmann B, Milucka J, Kuypers MMM. How low can they go? Aerobic respiration by microorganisms under apparent anoxia. FEMS Microbiol Rev. 2022;fuac006. 10.1093/femsre/fuac006.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC9075580</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">35094062</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Berg JS, Pjevac P, Sommer T, Buckner CRT, Philippi M, Hach PF, et al. Dark aerobic sulfide oxidation by anoxygenic phototrophs in anoxic waters. Environ Microbiol. 2019;21:1611&#x2013;26. doi: 10.1111/1462-2920.14543.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1111/1462-2920.14543</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30689286</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Stamenkovi&#x107; V, Ward LM, Mischna M, Fischer WW. O2 solubility in Martian near-surface environments and implications for aerobic life. Nat Geosci. 2018;11:905&#x2013;9. doi: 10.1038/s41561-018-0243-0.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41561-018-0243-0</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">37938692</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2023</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>10</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2730-6151</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>2</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2022</Year><Month>Mar</Month><Day>08</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>ISME communications</Title><ISOAbbreviation>ISME Commun</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Weakened resilience of benthic microbial communities in the face of climate change.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>21</StartPage><MedlinePgn>21</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">21</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s43705-022-00104-9</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Increased ocean temperature associated with climate change is especially intensified in coastal areas and its influence on microbial communities and biogeochemical cycling is poorly understood. In this study, we sampled a Baltic Sea bay that has undergone 50 years of warmer temperatures similar to RCP5-8.5 predictions due to cooling water release from a nuclear power plant. The system demonstrated reduced oxygen concentrations, decreased anaerobic electron acceptors, and higher rates of sulfate reduction. Chemical analyses, 16S rRNA gene amplicons, and RNA transcripts all supported sediment anaerobic reactions occurring closer to the sediment-water interface. This resulted in higher microbial diversities and raised sulfate reduction and methanogenesis transcripts, also supporting increased production of toxic sulfide and the greenhouse gas methane closer to the sediment surface, with possible release to oxygen deficient waters. RNA transcripts supported prolonged periods of cyanobacterial bloom that may result in increased climate change related coastal anoxia. Finally, while metatranscriptomics suggested increased energy production in the heated bay, a large number of stress transcripts indicated the communities had not adapted to the increased temperature and had weakened resilience. The results point to a potential feedback loop, whereby increased temperatures may amplify negative effects at the base of coastal biochemical cycling.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2022. The Author(s).</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Seidel</LastName><ForeName>Laura</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-2620-914X</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centre for ecology and evolution in microbial model systems (EEMiS), Linnaeus University, Kalmar, Sweden. laura.seidel@lnu.se.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ketzer</LastName><ForeName>Marcelo</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-4796-8177</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Biology and Environmental Science, Linnaeus University, Kalmar, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Broman</LastName><ForeName>Elias</ForeName><Initials>E</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-9005-5168</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ecology, Environment and Plant Sciences, Stockholm University, Stockholm, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Shahabi-Ghahfarokhi</LastName><ForeName>Sina</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Biology and Environmental Science, Linnaeus University, Kalmar, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Rahmati-Abkenar</LastName><ForeName>Mahboubeh</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-3193-9331</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Biology and Environmental Science, Linnaeus University, Kalmar, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Turner</LastName><ForeName>Stephanie</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centre for ecology and evolution in microbial model systems (EEMiS), Linnaeus University, Kalmar, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>St&#xe5;hle</LastName><ForeName>Magnus</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centre for ecology and evolution in microbial model systems (EEMiS), Linnaeus University, Kalmar, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Bergstr&#xf6;m</LastName><ForeName>Kristofer</ForeName><Initials>K</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centre for ecology and evolution in microbial model systems (EEMiS), Linnaeus University, Kalmar, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Manoharan</LastName><ForeName>Lokeshwaran</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-9751-5745</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Bioinformatics Infrastructure Sweden (NBIS), SciLifeLab, Division of Occupational and Environmental Medicine, Department of Laboratory Medicine, Lund University, Lund, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ali</LastName><ForeName>Ashfaq</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Bioinformatics Infrastructure Swe